1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,311
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:04,311 --> 00:00:08,563
Principles, Big Idea 1: Creative
Development. Let's see how real programs

3
00:00:08,563 --> 00:00:13,457
get planned, built, and fixed. After every
lesson, we'll work through a full example

4
00:00:13,457 --> 00:00:17,301
step by step, and we'll finish by putting
the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:22,379
Almost every app you use was built by a
team, not one person.

6
00:00:22,829 --> 00:00:27,672
In AP Computer Science Principles,
collaboration is the first big idea.

7
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:32,704
Teams build better programs because people
bring different perspectives.

8
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:37,480
Someone who thinks differently from you
will spot problems, and ideas, that you

9
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:38,082
would miss.

10
00:00:39,232 --> 00:00:42,155
One common way to work together is pair
programming.

11
00:00:42,605 --> 00:00:47,405
The driver types the code. The navigator
reviews each line and thinks ahead. Then

12
00:00:47,405 --> 00:00:48,472
they switch roles.

13
00:00:49,622 --> 00:00:53,163
Good collaboration also means getting
feedback from users.

14
00:00:53,613 --> 00:00:58,558
Interviews, surveys and testing with real
people show what the program actually

15
00:00:58,558 --> 00:00:59,309
needs to do.

16
00:01:00,459 --> 00:01:05,336
Teams use tools to share work: shared
documents, version control that tracks

17
00:01:05,336 --> 00:01:08,288
every change, and online tools to
communicate.

18
00:01:08,738 --> 00:01:12,792
Clear communication and respectful
disagreement are part of the job.

19
00:01:13,942 --> 00:01:17,611
On the AP exam, you might be asked why a
diverse team helps.

20
00:01:18,061 --> 00:01:22,626
The answer: different backgrounds and
perspectives help a team find errors and

21
00:01:22,626 --> 00:01:24,440
design for more kinds of users.

22
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:28,790
Your turn. In pair programming, what does
the navigator do?

23
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:30,434
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:30,884 --> 00:01:35,460
The navigator reviews the code as it's
typed, catches mistakes, and plans the

25
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,540
next steps, while the driver types.

26
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:40,977
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

27
00:01:42,127 --> 00:01:47,075
Let's follow a real pair programming
session, and see how collaboration catches

28
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:48,015
problems early.

29
00:01:49,165 --> 00:01:53,631
The problem: Maya and Leo are building a
quiz app together. Maya drives, Leo

30
00:01:53,631 --> 00:01:58,391
navigates. Over twenty minutes, what does
each of them do, and what does teamwork

31
00:01:58,391 --> 00:01:58,744
catch?

32
00:01:59,894 --> 00:02:04,890
The driver types the code. The navigator
reads every line, thinks ahead, and asks

33
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:07,296
questions. They switch roles regularly.

34
00:02:07,746 --> 00:02:12,397
They also ask other people for feedback,
like classmates who might use the app.

35
00:02:13,547 --> 00:02:19,129
Minute four: Leo notices the score starts
at one, not zero. Fixed before it ever

36
00:02:19,129 --> 00:02:19,478
runs.

37
00:02:19,928 --> 00:02:24,690
Minute twelve: now Leo drives. Maya
suggests showing the right answer after a

38
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:25,432
wrong guess.

39
00:02:25,882 --> 00:02:30,823
Testing: a classmate who is color blind
can't tell the red and green feedback

40
00:02:30,823 --> 00:02:33,775
apart. They add words: correct, and try
again.

41
00:02:34,925 --> 00:02:38,338
Three improvements, and none of them came
from the person typing.

42
00:02:38,788 --> 00:02:43,843
Different perspectives catch different
problems. That's why the AP exam counts

43
00:02:43,843 --> 00:02:45,658
collaboration as a big idea.

44
00:02:46,808 --> 00:02:51,455
Your turn. Name one benefit of asking
users from different backgrounds to test

45
00:02:51,455 --> 00:02:51,992
your app.

46
00:02:52,442 --> 00:02:53,636
Pause the video now.

47
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:58,614
They find problems you wouldn't, like
accessibility or language issues, so the

48
00:02:58,614 --> 00:03:00,124
app works for more people.

49
00:03:00,574 --> 00:03:02,472
Next, program function and purpose.

50
00:03:03,622 --> 00:03:05,713
Every program is made for a reason.

51
00:03:06,163 --> 00:03:10,400
Before you write code, you should be able
to say what the program is for, and what

52
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:10,813
it does.

53
00:03:11,963 --> 00:03:16,726
A program's purpose is the problem it
solves, or the creative thing it lets

54
00:03:16,726 --> 00:03:17,361
people do.

55
00:03:17,811 --> 00:03:21,864
A step counter's purpose is to help people
track how active they are.

56
00:03:23,014 --> 00:03:26,001
A program's function is how it behaves
when it runs.

57
00:03:26,451 --> 00:03:30,312
It's described by its inputs, what it does
with them, and its outputs.

58
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:37,034
Inputs can come from the user, like a tap,
a click, or typed text. They can also come

59
00:03:37,034 --> 00:03:39,590
from sensors, files, or other programs.

60
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:44,264
Programs that react to events, like a
button click, are called event driven.

61
00:03:45,414 --> 00:03:50,684
Outputs are what the program produces:
text on the screen, a sound, an image, a

62
00:03:50,684 --> 00:03:53,286
vibration, or data sent somewhere else.

63
00:03:53,736 --> 00:03:56,125
A program can have many inputs and
outputs.

64
00:03:57,275 --> 00:04:02,300
A code segment is a smaller part of a
program. You can describe what one segment

65
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:05,126
does, even without knowing the whole
program.

66
00:04:05,576 --> 00:04:09,309
On the exam, you'll often explain what a
short code segment does.

67
00:04:10,459 --> 00:04:14,321
Your turn. In a step counter app, name one
input and one output.

68
00:04:14,771 --> 00:04:15,965
Pause the video now.

69
00:04:16,415 --> 00:04:21,545
Input: the motion sensor, which detects
steps. Output: the step count shown on the

70
00:04:21,545 --> 00:04:21,983
screen.

71
00:04:22,433 --> 00:04:25,420
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

72
00:04:26,570 --> 00:04:32,128
Let's describe a real program the way the
AP exam wants: its purpose, its inputs,

73
00:04:32,128 --> 00:04:33,226
and its outputs.

74
00:04:34,376 --> 00:04:38,944
The problem: describe a reading log app.
Students type how many minutes they read

75
00:04:38,944 --> 00:04:43,400
each day, and the app shows their total
and whether they met their weekly goal.

76
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:47,985
Purpose is why the program exists: the
problem it solves.

77
00:04:48,435 --> 00:04:51,805
Function is what it does: inputs,
processing, and outputs.

78
00:04:52,955 --> 00:04:57,414
Try a week where the student types thirty,
forty five, twenty and fifty five.

79
00:04:57,864 --> 00:05:02,797
The program adds them: one hundred fifty.
That reaches the goal of one hundred

80
00:05:02,797 --> 00:05:03,176
fifty.

81
00:05:03,626 --> 00:05:08,511
Output: total one hundred fifty, average
thirty seven point five, and Goal met.

82
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:12,413
A clear description separates the why from
the what.

83
00:05:12,863 --> 00:05:17,592
On the Create task you'll describe your
own program's purpose and function, so

84
00:05:17,592 --> 00:05:20,138
practice saying both in one sentence each.

85
00:05:21,288 --> 00:05:26,573
Your turn. A weather app shows tomorrow's
forecast for your location. Name its input

86
00:05:26,573 --> 00:05:27,517
and its output.

87
00:05:27,967 --> 00:05:29,162
Pause the video now.

88
00:05:29,612 --> 00:05:32,705
Input: your location. Output: tomorrow's
forecast.

89
00:05:33,155 --> 00:05:35,203
Next, program design and development.

90
00:05:36,353 --> 00:05:38,337
Good programs aren't written all at once.

91
00:05:38,787 --> 00:05:42,158
They're designed, built, tested, and
improved, over and over.

92
00:05:43,308 --> 00:05:47,743
A development process has steps.
Investigate the problem. Design a

93
00:05:47,743 --> 00:05:51,372
solution. Build it. Test it. Then reflect
and improve.

94
00:05:51,822 --> 00:05:54,936
This cycle repeats, so it's called an
iterative process.

95
00:05:56,086 --> 00:06:00,956
In the investigate step, you find out what
users need, and write down the program's

96
00:06:00,956 --> 00:06:01,718
requirements.

97
00:06:02,168 --> 00:06:06,695
Requirements describe what the program
must do, and any limits it must work

98
00:06:06,695 --> 00:06:07,118
within.

99
00:06:08,268 --> 00:06:12,749
In the design step, you plan before you
code: sketches of the screens, a

100
00:06:12,749 --> 00:06:14,305
flowchart, or pseudocode.

101
00:06:14,755 --> 00:06:19,721
Big problems are split into smaller parts
that are easier to solve. That's called

102
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:20,579
decomposition.

103
00:06:21,729 --> 00:06:25,782
Then you build and test in small pieces,
an incremental process.

104
00:06:26,232 --> 00:06:29,262
Get one part working, test it, then add
the next part.

105
00:06:30,412 --> 00:06:35,219
Programmers also document their work with
comments, and give credit when they use

106
00:06:35,219 --> 00:06:36,406
someone else's code.

107
00:06:36,856 --> 00:06:41,404
Comments explain what the code does and
why, for other people and your future

108
00:06:41,404 --> 00:06:41,699
self.

109
00:06:42,849 --> 00:06:47,216
Your turn. You build a game by first
getting the player to move, testing it,

110
00:06:47,216 --> 00:06:51,468
then adding enemies, then adding scores.
What kind of development is that?

111
00:06:51,918 --> 00:06:53,112
Pause the video now.

112
00:06:53,562 --> 00:06:57,872
Incremental development: building and
testing one small piece at a time.

113
00:06:58,322 --> 00:07:01,308
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

114
00:07:02,459 --> 00:07:07,498
Let's take one feature through the whole
development cycle: investigate, design,

115
00:07:07,498 --> 00:07:09,072
build, test, and improve.

116
00:07:10,222 --> 00:07:14,649
The problem: students want the reading log
to tell them how many minutes they still

117
00:07:14,649 --> 00:07:15,876
need to reach the goal.

118
00:07:16,326 --> 00:07:18,118
Take this feature through every step.

119
00:07:19,268 --> 00:07:21,465
Each step produces something you can
check.

120
00:07:21,915 --> 00:07:26,416
The cycle repeats: testing often sends you
back to design, and that is normal.

121
00:07:27,566 --> 00:07:30,148
Test one hundred: short by fifty. Correct.

122
00:07:30,598 --> 00:07:35,291
Test one fifty: it says short by zero.
That's wrong. It should say goal met.

123
00:07:35,741 --> 00:07:40,621
The design used greater than instead of
greater than or equal. Change it, test

124
00:07:40,621 --> 00:07:42,248
again, and all three pass.

125
00:07:43,398 --> 00:07:47,067
One test found a bug, the design was
fixed, and the feature shipped.

126
00:07:47,517 --> 00:07:52,518
That's an iterative process: you go around
the cycle again whenever testing finds a

127
00:07:52,518 --> 00:07:52,000
problem.

128
00:07:54,150 --> 00:07:57,883
Your turn. Which step comes right after
testing finds a problem?

129
00:07:58,333 --> 00:07:59,528
Pause the video now.

130
00:07:59,978 --> 00:08:03,156
Go back and improve the design or code,
then test again.

131
00:08:03,606 --> 00:08:05,804
Next, identifying and correcting errors.

132
00:08:06,953 --> 00:08:11,156
Every programmer makes mistakes. Good
programmers know how to find them.

133
00:08:11,606 --> 00:08:14,699
There are four kinds of errors to know for
the AP exam.

134
00:08:15,849 --> 00:08:20,222
A syntax error breaks the rules of the
language, like a missing parenthesis.

135
00:08:20,672 --> 00:08:23,168
The program won't run at all until it's
fixed.

136
00:08:24,318 --> 00:08:28,222
A logic error means the program runs, but
gives the wrong result.

137
00:08:28,672 --> 00:08:32,619
For example, an average that adds the
numbers but forgets to divide.

138
00:08:33,769 --> 00:08:39,255
A run time error happens while the program
is running, like dividing by zero, or

139
00:08:39,255 --> 00:08:42,409
asking for item ten of a list with five
items.

140
00:08:42,859 --> 00:08:47,382
An overflow error happens when a number is
too big for the space it's stored in.

141
00:08:48,532 --> 00:08:53,766
To find errors, test with many inputs,
including edge cases like zero, negative

142
00:08:53,766 --> 00:08:55,422
numbers, and empty lists.

143
00:08:55,872 --> 00:09:00,493
Trace the code by hand, or add print
statements to see what's happening.

144
00:09:00,493 --> 00:09:03,702
Debuggers can also pause the program step
by step.

145
00:09:04,852 --> 00:09:08,201
For each test, know the expected output
before you run it.

146
00:09:08,651 --> 00:09:11,403
If the actual output is different, you've
found a bug.

147
00:09:12,553 --> 00:09:17,656
Your turn. A program to find the largest
number starts with max equals zero. It

148
00:09:17,656 --> 00:09:22,952
works for five, nine, two, but gives zero
for negative three, negative seven. What

149
00:09:22,952 --> 00:09:24,244
kind of error is it?

150
00:09:24,694 --> 00:09:25,888
Pause the video now.

151
00:09:26,338 --> 00:09:31,295
A logic error. The program runs, but max
should start at the first item in the

152
00:09:31,295 --> 00:09:32,248
list, not zero.

153
00:09:32,698 --> 00:09:35,684
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

154
00:09:36,835 --> 00:09:41,571
Let's look at four buggy versions of the
same program, and name each kind of error.

155
00:09:42,721 --> 00:09:48,141
The problem: each version of the reading
log has one bug. Is it a syntax, logic,

156
00:09:48,141 --> 00:09:50,038
run time, or overflow error?

157
00:09:51,188 --> 00:09:56,876
Syntax: the program won't run. Logic: it
runs, but the answer is wrong. Run time:

158
00:09:56,876 --> 00:10:01,791
it crashes while running. Overflow: a
number is too big for its space.

159
00:10:02,941 --> 00:10:07,604
Version A: a missing quote breaks the
language rules. It won't run at all.

160
00:10:07,604 --> 00:10:08,424
Syntax error.

161
00:10:08,874 --> 00:10:13,268
Version B: it runs, but the average is
just the total. Logic error.

162
00:10:13,718 --> 00:10:18,156
Version C: dividing by zero crashes while
running. Run time error.

163
00:10:18,606 --> 00:10:23,719
Version D: four bits only hold up to
fifteen. One hundred fifty doesn't fit.

164
00:10:23,719 --> 00:10:24,728
Overflow error.

165
00:10:25,878 --> 00:10:26,881
One of each type.

166
00:10:27,331 --> 00:10:32,468
To find logic errors, use test cases with
known answers, print statements, and hand

167
00:10:32,468 --> 00:10:32,963
tracing.

168
00:10:34,113 --> 00:10:39,017
Your turn. A program is supposed to find
the largest number, but it always returns

169
00:10:39,017 --> 00:10:41,409
the first one. What kind of error is it?

170
00:10:41,859 --> 00:10:43,054
Pause the video now.

171
00:10:43,504 --> 00:10:46,426
A logic error. It runs, but the result is
wrong.

172
00:10:46,876 --> 00:10:48,924
Next, let's put Big Idea one together.

173
00:10:50,074 --> 00:10:54,426
You've learned collaboration, program
purpose and function, the development

174
00:10:54,426 --> 00:10:55,877
process, and error types.

175
00:10:56,327 --> 00:11:01,145
Now let's look at the finished reading
log, a complete program that really runs,

176
00:11:01,145 --> 00:11:03,132
and see where each idea shows up.

177
00:11:04,282 --> 00:11:09,388
Here's the plan. The program is written in
Python, but every idea matches AP

178
00:11:09,388 --> 00:11:10,127
pseudocode.

179
00:11:10,577 --> 00:11:14,460
The comments record what testing found,
just like a real team would.

180
00:11:15,610 --> 00:11:19,194
Part one: the comments state the purpose,
inputs and outputs.

181
00:11:19,644 --> 00:11:25,151
Two procedures. average guards against
dividing by zero, a run time error found

182
00:11:25,151 --> 00:11:30,589
in testing. goal message uses greater than
or equal, fixing the logic error at

183
00:11:30,589 --> 00:11:32,401
exactly one hundred fifty.

184
00:11:33,551 --> 00:11:37,754
Part two: a list collects the minutes
until the user enters a blank line.

185
00:11:38,204 --> 00:11:42,904
Then the program outputs the total, the
average and the goal message, using the

186
00:11:42,904 --> 00:11:43,559
procedures.

187
00:11:44,709 --> 00:11:48,869
Let's run it with the test week: thirty,
forty five, twenty, fifty five.

188
00:11:49,319 --> 00:11:54,054
One hundred fifty minutes, an average of
thirty seven point five, and the goal is

189
00:11:54,054 --> 00:11:56,743
met, right on the boundary that testing
fixed.

190
00:11:57,893 --> 00:12:02,359
Your turn. The user presses enter right
away, with no days. What are the last

191
00:12:02,359 --> 00:12:03,055
three lines?

192
00:12:03,505 --> 00:12:04,700
Pause the video now.

193
00:12:05,150 --> 00:12:09,975
Total zero, average zero thanks to the
guard, and short by one hundred fifty

194
00:12:09,975 --> 00:12:10,483
minutes.

195
00:12:10,933 --> 00:12:14,069
That's Big Idea one. Next, Big Idea two:
data.

196
00:12:15,219 --> 00:12:19,648
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

197
00:12:19,648 --> 00:12:20,041
lesson.

198
00:12:20,491 --> 00:12:21,323
See you there.

